
# Workstation IP Address Troubleshooting

## Overview
Investigated an unexpected IP address assignment
while working with a network switch and wireless
access point installation.

## Environment
- Office Ethernet Infrastructure
- TP-Link PoE Switch
- Wireless Access Point
- Windows Workstation
- Network-Connected Desk Phone

## Initial Problem
A workstation received an unexpected IP address
after changes to the network connection.

The computer also displayed an unexpected network
name, raising questions about which network
was providing its connectivity.

## Troubleshooting Process
1. Reviewed physical Ethernet connections.
2. Examined connections between the network
   outlet, PoE switch, and wireless access point.
3. Inspected the workstation's network information.
4. Tested connectivity using network diagnostics.
5. Investigated the workstation's network path.
6. Identified the desk phone as an important
   part of the workstation's connection.
7. Discovered that the ethernet cable run went to the switch connected to the Comcast Router instead of the usual switch that leads to the AT&T Router.

## Key Finding
The workstation connected through a network desk
phone rather than directly to the wall outlet.

This introduced another device into the network
path that needed to be considered when
investigating the unexpected IP assignment.

Certain runs can lead to different switches connected to different routers.

## Lessons Learned
- Always verify the complete physical network path.
- Desk phones may provide Ethernet passthrough.
- Voice and data networks may use different VLANs.
- Unexpected IP addresses can indicate differences
  in DHCP or network configuration.
- Physical topology matters during troubleshooting.

## Skills Demonstrated
- Network Troubleshooting
- Ethernet Connectivity
